sec('scope','Scope, evidence and notation',r'''
<p class="lead">Adversarial literature review and prior-art audit of P01–P14 · 4 October 2026</p>
<p>This report uses only the two pasted attachments as evidence of the project’s contents: the <em>Claim Blueprint: P01–P14</em> and the detailed review request. No project folders or proof files were inspected, and no project source files were changed. “Verified” below means verified <em>according to the blueprint</em>; this is a priority audit, not a fresh verification of the underlying proofs.</p>
<p><strong>Current:</strong> P01–P05 and P07–P11. <strong>Future / not yet proved:</strong> P06 and P12–P14. The scope ends at P14. The literature cutoff is this audit date; a search result’s crawl date was not treated as its publication date.</p>
<div class="math">DM: ẋᵢ = axᵢ − kxᵢ² + cxᵢ₋₁/(1+xᵢ) − gxᵢ/(1+xᵢ₊q) − hxᵢxᵢ₋q.<br>MB: ẏᵢ = ayᵢ − kyᵢ² + cyᵢyᵢ₋₁ − gyᵢyᵢ₊q − hyᵢyᵢ₋q.</div>
<p>Throughout, n≥5, 1≤q&lt;n, all five coefficients are positive, and indices are modular. At positive DM synchrony x, put s=1+x, C=c/s, G=gx/s², H=hx and d=−kx−c(1+2x)/s². Then rₘ=d+C cosθₘ+(G−H)cos(qθₘ), with θₘ=2πm/n. “Selected” always retains the complete set of maximizers of rₘ, including ties.</p>
<p><strong>Classification discipline.</strong> Each substantive claim receives one of the seven requested labels. A module can contain claims in different classes; the final table identifies its main content, rather than replacing the claim-level analysis. “APPARENTLY NEW” means no materially equivalent theorem was found in the inspected literature. It is conditional on unresolved exact-model sources. Confidence rates the judgment supported by available evidence; priority risk rates the remaining chance of a relevant antecedent. HIGH confidence that a method is routine does not mean its exact DM formula has already been printed.</p>
<p>Bibliography entries distinguish full-text inspection, selected equation/theorem inspection, and abstract/metadata access. Numbered citations link directly to primary papers, author copies or publication records. The large comparison table is searchable and horizontally scrollable.</p>
''')

sec('part1','PART I — Executive novelty verdict',r'''
<p class="lead">The project remains a plausible mathematical paper, but its defensible contribution is a constrained classification of modes and interaction-law contrasts—not Fourier analysis, the golden ratio, or the generic existence of rotating waves.</p>
<p>The strongest current package is P05 + P11 + P08. P05 identifies every finite-ring mode that can maximize the real spectrum. P11 then cuts down that list using positivity and the equilibrium relation: some realizable dominant cells cannot contain a physical spectral boundary at all. P08 asks how these possibilities change for a specified bilinear comparator at the same nominal coefficient tuple. The resulting parity and branch restrictions are substantially more informative than a numerical demonstration that two nonlinearities behave differently.</p>
<p>The exact P11 iff criterion and the P08 all-size, branch-labelled comparison theorems are the leading apparent novelty candidates. No materially equivalent theorem was found in the primary sources inspected. This is a <strong>MEDIUM-confidence, UNRESOLVED-priority-risk</strong> judgment, because the closest five-element proceedings and thesis are not fully accessible. It is not a certificate of first publication. The positive-parameter lift of P05’s cells and switching surfaces is a meaningful supporting result. The bare nearest-cosine calculation is much less distinctive.</p>
<p>A close precedent is the Lorenz-96 equation ẋᵢ=xᵢ₋₁(xᵢ₊₁−xᵢ₋₂)−xᵢ+F. Its homogeneous-state real spectrum is −1+F(cosθ−cos2θ). For F&gt;0, writing u=cosθ yields a concave quadratic with vertex 1/4. The published analysis includes finite-size first-Hopf selection, simultaneous critical pairs and explicit nonlinear criticality. This directly precludes selling a quadratic cosine dispersion or a finite-ring Hopf calculation as a new general method. The relevant locations are Lemma 1, Theorems 2/4 and Proposition 5 in the inspected preprint. {B08}</p>
<p>Our additional task is different: the DM vertex moves with positive physical parameters, while its diagonal term and equilibrium are constrained by the same parameters. A freely movable quadratic vertex does not establish that all cells lift to positive coefficients, nor that a selected cell can reach r=0. Those are the mathematical distinctions that should carry the paper. P11 expresses the latter obstruction explicitly through wⱼ&gt;0 and max(0,βⱼ)&lt;wⱼ/[4(1−uⱼ)]. The exclusions (8,1) and (12,2) are useful because they separate two notions of realizability within the same proven selection atlas.</p>
<p>Even the conceptual distinction between an algebraic Hopf point and a physically feasible one has prior art. Pais, Caicedo-Núñez and Leonard explicitly discuss infeasible Hopf parameter values for circulant replicator-mutator systems after Theorem 4.5; their Lemma 4.6 also evaluates a Fourier-structured Lyapunov coefficient. This prevents a “first distinction between mathematical and admissible Hopf modes” claim. It does not supply the DM cellwise necessity-and-sufficiency theorem. {B23}</p>
<p>P08 has the strongest independent narrative. Its universal forward witness has x*=y*=1, so even equilibrium amplitude is matched at that point. More decisively, the reverse contrast on the specified unique-root domain exists exactly when n≡3 mod 4, while allowing the lower DM branch changes the obstruction to divisibility by four. The independent realization of every ordered pair of reachable dominant representatives with both equilibria stable further separates mode ordering from instability. These exact quantifiers and domain distinctions are what make P08 more than an example.</p>
<p>However, “same topology” must mean the same <em>unsigned directed dependency support</em> in this comparison. At positive states, the DM derivative with respect to xᵢ₊q is positive, while its MB analogue is negative. At q=2 these are distinct edges; for coincident offsets elsewhere, total derivatives must be combined. The comparison changes effective regulatory signs as well as receiver dependence and boundary behavior. Consequently the conclusions establish a property of these two specified laws, not an isolated biological effect of saturation with an unchanged signed interaction graph. Fixing nominal coefficients also presupposes the declared dimensionless state and time conventions.</p>
<p>P10 is the strongest supporting steady-state theorem. The useful calculation is Gf−Hf=rf(k+c/sf²)&gt;0 at every positive fold, for every allowed n,q. That identity forces all transverse real parts strictly negative without extra parameter hypotheses. The subsequent simple-zero fold theorem, local sink–saddle pair, persistence of sinks and local separatrix use established machinery. Broader cyclic-feedback literature already studies bistability and basin organization; its single-input monotonicity assumptions do not automatically hold for DM. {B25} {B34}</p>
<p>The remaining current modules should mostly support those headline results. P01 establishes a sound phase space and bounds. Its exact-radius finite-entry iff statement is a small model-specific refinement; the maximum comparison method is routine, and the blueprint expressly does not claim an optimal radius. P02 is exact root algebra on synchrony. P03–P04 are differentiation and standard circulant spectral theory. P07 is a useful five-node worked example, especially for stable ties, but fifth-root golden-ratio identities are not a new biological principle. An earlier five-agent Wu-Xing paper explicitly emphasizes golden-ratio structure. {B06} {B17}</p>
<p>P09’s full-system transcritical statement is stronger than a scalar sketch, but its ingredients are an invariant center line, a simple uniform zero and a transverse implicit-function argument. That makes it a correct routine application rather than a new bifurcation mechanism. The degenerate cubic locus should stay separately stated. P10 does not justify stability of the upper branch, index one of the lower branch, or exclusivity of two attractors throughout the entire two-root interval; the blueprint already contains an exact counterexample.</p>
<p>Future P12–P14 have unequal value. A nonzero total crossing derivative on the frozen g-path is necessary infrastructure. A nonzero first Lyapunov coefficient converts the spectral candidate into a classified nonlinear local bifurcation and can materially improve completeness, even if the calculation itself adds little methodological novelty. The best P13 target is an explicit sign theorem on a substantial admissible part of the Hopf surface, indexed by size or mode and connected to the P11 restrictions. The existence of some small open neighborhood of one nonzero value follows from continuity; that alone is not a strong region theorem. Fourier contraction through modes 0 and 2m already appears in generalized Lorenz-96 work. {B11} {B21}</p>
<p>Once a simple nondegenerate Hopf branch is proved, P14’s exact cyclic phase relation, cluster count and signed propagation are essentially consequences of equivariant Hopf theory. Stewart’s recent ring treatment gives both general phase-pattern statements and a direction criterion. The contribution of this project would be identifying which physically feasible DM modes actually produce those waves and on which parameter side, rather than introducing a new rotating-wave theorem. {B12} {B16}</p>
<p>The biggest priority issue remains the Lin–Shi–Han line. A 2020 bilinear five-element equation was inspected; it is not DM. The accessible later manuscript excerpt uses enzyme activation/inactivation fractions, again visibly different in those terms. But the 2021–2023 proceedings, the complete 2024 main text and Lin’s 2021 master’s thesis have not all been checked. The retrieved 2024 supplement refers to a Hopf parameter region, so “those papers are only numerical” is not defensible. The proper overall exact-model verdict is <strong>PRIORITY STATUS UNRESOLVED</strong>. {B01} {B02} {B03} {B04} {B05}</p>
<p>My recommended paper story is: <strong>“Exact finite-ring mode selection and physical criticality constraints under two specified interaction laws.”</strong> It can connect dominance, admissible criticality, same-tuple contrasts and the steady-state branch structure in a coherent theorem sequence. Publication-worthiness is conditional on the underlying proofs and the remaining priority checks; it should not be inferred from the sheer number of modules. A compact paper led by P08 and P11, with P05 as their common geometric foundation and P10 as branch organization, is more persuasive than fourteen equally weighted novelty claims.</p>
''')

sec('part2','PART II — Exact-model priority audit',r'''
<p><strong>Verdict: PRIORITY STATUS UNRESOLVED.</strong> I did not find a verified earlier occurrence of the complete canonical DM equation. I also did not obtain enough of the closest proceedings/thesis chain to certify its absence. Exact-model priority and theorem priority are separate: a new equation does not make Fourier diagonalization new; an old equation could still support new constrained classifications.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>What is established by inspection</th><th>What remains unresolved</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Lin–Han 2020 {B01}</td><td>Eq. (2), printed p. 778, is bilinear: axᵢ−bxᵢ²+cxᵢxᵢ₋₁−dxᵢxᵢ₋₂−g₀xᵢxᵢ₊₁−hxᵢxᵢ₊₂, for five nodes.</td><td>Do not describe its “first Lyapunov method” as a computed Hopf first Lyapunov coefficient. Those are different concepts.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lin–Shi–Han 2021 {B02}</td><td>Title, authors, DOI, conference and pages confirmed.</td><td>Actual denominator, equation, equilibrium/spectrum and Hopf analysis require the paper.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lin–Shi–Han 2022 {B03}</td><td>Proceedings metadata confirmed.</td><td>The ResearchGate record exposed a later two-author manuscript. That is not a verified copy of the 2022 three-author paper.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lin–Han 2023 {B04}</td><td>Multi-node paper and bibliographic record confirmed.</td><td>How much analytical n-dependence, normal-form analysis or model comparison it contains is unknown.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lin–Han 2024 {B05}</td><td>Publisher record plus partial author manuscript and public supplement inspected. Visible enzymatic law has activation aXₘ(1−Xᵢ)/(1−Xᵢ+K) and inactivation −bXₙXᵢ/(Xᵢ+k).</td><td>The complete main text, including its Hopf section and all equations, is needed. No reliable exclusion of an l₁ calculation can be made.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lin 2021 thesis</td><td>Official laboratory list confirms a July 2021 master’s thesis, supervisor Jing Han: <em>多子系统生克耦合模型的研究</em>.</td><td>No thesis full text obtained. Potentially the most detailed antecedent; degree level is master’s, not PhD.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Xiong–Zhang 2025 {B32}</td><td>Journal abstract describes simulated enzymatic five-element oscillations; references point to the thesis and Lin’s 2021/2024 papers.</td><td>Full equation and possible analytic results not inspected. Website dates conflict internally; cite the journal’s 2025 volume/issue.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Exact bilinear mapping.</strong> In the displayed 2020 law, set g₀=0, identify b with current k, old d with current h, and old h with current g. This gives the current n=5,q=2 MB equation at a boundary of the six-term coefficient family. It does not give DM, and it is not the identity mapping of same-named coefficients. This source comparison does not upgrade the blueprint’s deferred six-term spectrum into a verified project theorem. {B01}</p>
<p><strong>Receiver dependence is not enough for identity.</strong> The visible 2024 enzymatic factors depend on the receiver’s active/inactive fraction. DM’s c-term has a source numerator and receiver denominator without the inactive-fraction numerator; its g-term has the denominator on a different node. Those are mathematical differences in derivatives and boundary behavior, not interchangeable descriptions of “saturation.” Affine state changes, reversal of ring orientation or parameter renaming must be checked term by term before claiming equivalence. {B05}</p>
<p><strong>Supplement successfully retrieved.</strong> The publisher link delivers a 7z archive, despite the page’s PDF-style label. Its <code>supplementary.tex</code> contains five sections: sampling, five-node topology enumeration, period/amplitude in Hopf parameter space, analytical adaptation, and topology simplification. Section 3 explicitly refers to the main paper’s Figure 12 Hopf region. No first-Lyapunov formula was found in the inspected supplementary source. This says nothing definitive about the inaccessible main Hopf section. <a href="https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1007%2Fs11071-024-09374-5/MediaObjects/11071_2024_9374_MOESM1_ESM.7z">Publisher supplement</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Historical context.</strong> Earlier five-element simulations and a linear five-agent golden-ratio analysis establish that the motivating graph and its fifth-root constants are not new. Neither is verified here as an exact antecedent of the DM positivity-constrained theorem package. {B07} {B06}</p>
<p>Required library packet: the four main papers B02–B05, Lin’s thesis, and the complete 2025 article. For each, extract the precise law, state domain, homogeneous coefficient assumptions, n/q range, equilibrium equation, characteristic spectrum, admissible parameter restrictions, crossing derivative, l₁ formula and wave statement. Until this packet is checked, use “to our knowledge, within the sources reviewed” for specific theorems and no unqualified first-model claim. <a href="https://lsc.amss.cas.cn/rcpy/bfbys/202601/t20260122_821421.html">Official thesis listing</a>.</p>
''')

sec('part3','PART III — Module-by-module P01–P14 review',r'''
<article id="p01"><h3>P01 — Positivity, global existence and absorption · CURRENT</h3>
<p><strong>Current result.</strong> Global nonnegative flow; a Dini-maximum comparison gives an invariant attracting cube Kᵣ and explicit entry estimates. The weaker-sign extension distinguishes attraction from finite entry into the exact radius and gives an iff condition. The step-one input propagates positivity without a coprimality requirement.</p>
<p><strong>Known result / overlap.</strong> Quasipositivity, local smoothness, continuation under bounds and scalar maximum comparisons are standard. Repressilator bounding arguments and cyclic-feedback compact-attractor arguments supply nearby applications, not the same DM bound. {B22} {B25}</p>
<p><strong>Minimal difference.</strong> The rational receiver term produces a particular scalar majorant; retaining losses yields a useful exact-radius finite-entry classification. The first difference is routine, the latter technical and model-specific. <strong>Labels:</strong> core ROUTINE APPLICATION (HIGH / LOW); exact-radius iff MODEL-SPECIFIC EXTENSION (MEDIUM / MODERATE). <strong>Treatment:</strong> short foundational proposition; extended edge cases in an appendix. Do not call R optimal, and do not equate an attracting set with a minimal global attractor.</p></article>

<article id="p02"><h3>P02 — Synchronous equilibria · CURRENT</h3>
<p><strong>Current result.</strong> Synchrony reduces exactly to f(s)=s(μ+bs−As²)/(1+s), A=k+h, b=a−A, μ=a+c−g. The positive-root classification includes two roots precisely when a&gt;A and a+c&lt;g&lt;a+c+(a−A)²/(4A).</p>
<p><strong>Known result / overlap.</strong> Invariant synchrony is covered by symmetry/coupled-cell theory; solving the resulting quadratic and checking its admissible domain is elementary. {B14} {B15}</p>
<p><strong>Minimal difference.</strong> The coefficients and parameter interval belong to this law; the reduction and algebra are not a new method. Synchrony invariance: KNOWN MORE GENERALLY (HIGH / LOW). Exact root table: ROUTINE APPLICATION (HIGH / MODERATE for exact publication priority). <strong>Treatment:</strong> compact branch diagram and root proposition. Full negative-root/domain bookkeeping belongs in the appendix. This classifies synchronous equilibria only, not all equilibria or their full-system stability.</p></article>

<article id="p03"><h3>P03 — Origin and homogeneous Jacobian · CURRENT</h3>
<p><strong>Current result.</strong> J₀=(a−g)I+cP, uniform abscissa μ, exact homogeneous coefficients and correct addition of coincident offsets.</p>
<p><strong>Known result / overlap.</strong> Evaluating a polynomial in the cyclic shift on roots of unity is standard circulant theory. {B17}</p>
<p><strong>Minimal difference.</strong> Differentiating the rational law, including the positive g-denominator derivative, and respecting offset collisions are model-specific calculations. ROUTINE APPLICATION (HIGH / LOW for method; exact-model priority unresolved). <strong>Treatment:</strong> one transparent Jacobian lemma. Preserve the sign warning and state that a nonuniform imaginary-axis locus at the origin lies in an already unstable region because the uniform mode has larger real part. Do not promote such a locus to the first loss of stability.</p></article>

<article id="p04"><h3>P04 — Spectrum, normality and semigroup · CURRENT</h3>
<p><strong>Current result.</strong> Every homogeneous Jacobian is unitarily Fourier diagonalizable, normal and semisimple, with exact real/imaginary parts and ‖exp(tJ)‖₂=exp(t max rₘ).</p>
<p><strong>Known result / overlap.</strong> Chapter 3 of Gray supplies the eigenbasis for arbitrary circulants. The semigroup identity follows immediately by applying exp to the unitary diagonalization and taking its largest singular value. This is not a special nonlinear-network theorem. {B17}</p>
<p><strong>Minimal difference.</strong> Only substitution of d,C,G,H and the declared Fourier sign convention. Generic diagonalization, normality, multiplicity and norm conclusions: KNOWN MORE GENERALLY (HIGH / LOW). Specific dispersion: ROUTINE APPLICATION (HIGH / LOW). <strong>Treatment:</strong> framework lemma. Distinguish a Fourier label, an eigenvalue, a real-part tie and a repeated numerical value. A general nonsynchronous Jacobian need not retain normality.</p></article>

<article id="p05"><h3>P05 — Exact finite-n, q=2 selection · CURRENT</h3>
<p><strong>Current result.</strong> With T=G−H, the real dispersion is d−T+Cu+2Tu². The complete cell atlas has representatives 0 through J=⌊(n+1)/4⌋, adjacent midpoint ties, parity-sensitive terminal behavior, physical realizability and stable switching paths.</p>
<p><strong>Closest literature.</strong> The Lorenz-96 quadratic is the fixed-vertex comparison described in Part I. General unconstrained ring ordering is covered by Stewart’s Theorem 5.5/Corollary 5.6; it allows arbitrary connection coefficients and does not enforce the sparse positive DM law. {B08} {B12}</p>
<p><strong>Minimal difference / labels.</strong> Quadratic maximization and midpoint rule: ROUTINE APPLICATION (HIGH / LOW). Complete finite-size/parity atlas: MODEL-SPECIFIC EXTENSION (MEDIUM / MODERATE). Positive physical cell and switch realization: APPARENTLY NEW (MEDIUM / UNRESOLVED). Stable-mode switches: MODEL-SPECIFIC EXTENSION (MEDIUM / MODERATE), with little standalone novelty once the constructive chart is given. <strong>Treatment:</strong> main theorem, but lead with constrained realization and the P11 comparison rather than the elementary maximizer formula.</p></article>

<article id="p06"><h3>P06 — Large-n limit · FUTURE / NOT YET PROVED</h3>
<p><strong>Planned result.</strong> m*/n tends to arccos(u*)/(2π), with an anticipated O(n⁻²) eigenvalue discretization error.</p>
<p><strong>Known result / overlap.</strong> Lorenz-96 has a selected-wavenumber/limiting-period analysis; approximation of a smooth interior maximum by a refining grid is elementary. {B09}</p>
<p><strong>Minimal difference.</strong> A variable but fixed admissible DM vertex and its constants. Projected classification: ROUTINE APPLICATION (HIGH / LOW). <strong>Treatment:</strong> optional corollary/appendix. Specify the coefficient/equilibrium sequence, clip a vertex outside [−1,1], and distinguish O(1/n) normalized-wavenumber accuracy from O(n⁻²) objective error. A ring-size limit is not a derived continuum PDE. No result is currently established here.</p></article>

<article id="p07"><h3>P07 — Five-node and golden-ratio structure · CURRENT</h3>
<p><strong>Current result.</strong> Exact eigenvalues and seven orderings, pair {2,3} excluded from dominance, switch (H−G)/C=φ⁻², zero-frequency locus, semisimple coincidences and a Hurwitz dominant tie.</p>
<p><strong>Known result / overlap.</strong> Fifth roots of unity give the golden-ratio trigonometric constants; Yang’s five-agent study already makes golden-ratio claims in a related linear graph. It is not the same nonlinear stability theorem. {B06}</p>
<p><strong>Minimal difference / labels.</strong> Eigenvalues, ordering table and coincidence solving: ROUTINE APPLICATION (HIGH / MODERATE). Automatic stability of the physical dominant tie: MODEL-SPECIFIC EXTENSION (MEDIUM / UNRESOLVED), useful as a concrete obstruction to mistaking a switch for a bifurcation. “Golden ratio discovered in five-element network spectra”: FALSE NOVELTY (HIGH / HIGH). <strong>Treatment:</strong> worked example and candidate illustration; retain detailed collision cases in an appendix. No physiological golden-ratio inference follows.</p></article>

<article id="p08"><h3>P08 — Matched bilinear comparison · CURRENT</h3>
<p><strong>Current result.</strong> Exact MB equilibrium/growth and Fourier law; universal same-tuple forward stability contrast; distinct reverse-contrast theorems on unique-root and branch-labelled domains; independent stable realization of all ordered mode pairs.</p>
<p><strong>Known result / overlap.</strong> Circulant Lotka–Volterra spectra and Lyapunov regions are directly present in Wildenberg et al. §4, Eqs. (13)–(18). General interaction-law dependence of patterning predates this project. {B19} {B27}</p>
<p><strong>Minimal difference / labels.</strong> Logistic synchrony, spectrum and quadratic MB cells: ROUTINE APPLICATION (HIGH / LOW). Spectral sufficient dissipation via a symmetric part: KNOWN MORE GENERALLY (HIGH / LOW). The all-n witness, exact reverse parity/branch restrictions and independent ordered-pair realization: APPARENTLY NEW (MEDIUM / UNRESOLVED). <strong>Treatment:</strong> headline comparison theorem with explicit quantifiers and unsigned-support qualification. MB Hopf criticality is deferred. The nominal parameter matching is not a claim of equal equilibria, Jacobians or physical calibration.</p></article>

<article id="p09"><h3>P09 — Full-system transcritical and cubic degeneracy · CURRENT</h3>
<p><strong>Current result.</strong> At g=a+c, b≠0 gives a full-system transcritical; the local equilibrium IFT excludes nonsynchronous branches. At b=0 the center field is cubic and small branches scale as ±√(μ/A).</p>
<p><strong>Known result / overlap.</strong> A simple zero, invariant one-dimensional center manifold and nonzero parameter/quadratic coefficients are standard transcritical hypotheses. The transverse gap here is explicitly c[1−cos(2π/n)]. {B35}</p>
<p><strong>Minimal difference.</strong> The exact reduction and coefficients for this equation, rather than a new local bifurcation type. Generic transcritical: ROUTINE APPLICATION (HIGH / MODERATE). Cubic-degenerate calculations: MODEL-SPECIFIC EXTENSION (MEDIUM / MODERATE), with modest novelty. <strong>Treatment:</strong> supporting theorem joining the origin to the fold. Do not label the degenerate configuration a proved pitchfork/cusp or a completed two-parameter unfolding. Negative branches in the open mathematical domain must be separated from the nonnegative physical domain.</p></article>

<article id="p10"><h3>P10 — Universal positive fold and coexistence · CURRENT</h3>
<p><strong>Current result.</strong> Every positive fold with a&gt;k+h is a nondegenerate full-system saddle-node; its transverse spectrum is automatically stable. There is a near-fold two-sink region and an index-one lower saddle, plus a local basin separator.</p>
<p><strong>Known result / overlap.</strong> The generic fold theorem is standard; cyclic-feedback bistability is well studied under other hypotheses. {B34} {B25}</p>
<p><strong>Minimal difference / labels.</strong> The identity Gf−Hf&gt;0, the all-(n,q) transverse estimate and constructive sufficient coexistence interval form a MODEL-SPECIFIC EXTENSION (MEDIUM / UNRESOLVED). Applying fold, continuation and stable-manifold arguments: ROUTINE APPLICATION (HIGH / LOW). <strong>Treatment:</strong> supporting main theorem emphasizing the structural identity. The exact counterexample to whole-interval stability belongs in the main discussion; root count is not full-system bistability. The synchronous-ray basin threshold is global only on that ray; the separating disk is local.</p></article>

<article id="p11"><h3>P11 — Physical spectral criticality · CURRENT</h3>
<p><strong>Current result.</strong> Strict nonuniform selection with rⱼ=0 and ωⱼ≠0 yields one simple imaginary pair and stable complement. The key additional theorem is the exact positivity-constrained realizability iff, plus the five-node algebraic candidate.</p>
<p><strong>Closest literature.</strong> Feasibility filtering of circulant Hopf points appears explicitly in Pais et al.; structural Hopf exclusions also exist beyond rings. {B23} {B24}</p>
<p><strong>Minimal difference / labels.</strong> Single-pair implication, resolvents and normalized adjoints: ROUTINE APPLICATION (HIGH / LOW). DM cellwise iff and its obstruction cells: APPARENTLY NEW (MEDIUM / UNRESOLVED). Exact five-node candidate: MODEL-SPECIFIC EXTENSION (MEDIUM / UNRESOLVED), a valuable reproducible witness rather than an independent headline. <strong>Treatment:</strong> headline theorem immediately after P05. “First physical-versus-algebraic Hopf distinction”: FALSE NOVELTY (HIGH / HIGH). Do not call this a completed Hopf bifurcation or a first loss of stability.</p></article>

<article id="p12"><h3>P12 — Transversality · FUTURE / NOT YET PROVED</h3>
<p><strong>Target.</strong> The nonzero total derivative of Re λ₁ along (aH,kH,2,g,6), including motion of the upper equilibrium.</p>
<p><strong>Known result / overlap.</strong> Differentiating the equilibrium equation and eigenvalue along a smooth branch is standard Hopf verification. Generic nondegeneracy in a larger family does not prove it on this particular path. {B13} {B33}</p>
<p><strong>Minimal difference.</strong> Exact value/sign at this algebraic tuple. Projected ROUTINE APPLICATION (HIGH / LOW methodological risk). <strong>Treatment:</strong> lemma within a Hopf theorem. An explicit stable interval on one side follows locally after the crossing and gap are certified; “first” from a remote parameter starting point requires a separate global-in-parameter argument.</p></article>

<article id="p13"><h3>P13 — Nonlinear Hopf criticality · FUTURE / NOT YET PROVED</h3>
<p><strong>Target.</strong> Derive DM second/third derivatives, Fourier contractions, a normalized l₁ and its sign.</p>
<p><strong>Known result / overlap.</strong> The generic tensor formula is established. In a closely related cyclic calculation, Kerin–Engler’s Lemma 1/Eq. (29) reduces it through Fourier modes 0 and 2k; Page–Perez-Carrasco and Bușe et al. provide biological-ring criticality benchmarks. {B11} {B21} {B22}</p>
<p><strong>Minimal difference / labels.</strong> Generic formula and mode-index addition: KNOWN MORE GENERALLY (HIGH / LOW). One exact DM value: projected ROUTINE APPLICATION (MEDIUM / UNRESOLVED exact priority). A substantive admissible-region sign classification: projected MODEL-SPECIFIC EXTENSION (MEDIUM / UNRESOLVED), possibly stronger depending on scope. <strong>Treatment:</strong> main theorem only after proof; put long derivatives in an appendix. A vanished l₁ alone is not a Bautin theorem.</p></article>

<article id="p14"><h3>P14 — Exact nonlinear rotating waves · FUTURE / NOT YET PROVED</h3>
<p><strong>Target.</strong> Cyclic spatiotemporal symmetry, phase clusters and signed propagation on the Hopf branch.</p>
<p><strong>Known result / overlap.</strong> Equivariant Hopf supplies the exact phase relation, not merely its leading cosine approximation. Modern ring treatments include direction and long-range coupling. {B12} {B16} {B30}</p>
<p><strong>Minimal difference.</strong> Substitution of the physically realized DM mode and its crossing/criticality data. General wave consequence: KNOWN MORE GENERALLY (HIGH / LOW). DM application: projected ROUTINE APPLICATION (HIGH / LOW methodological risk). <strong>Treatment:</strong> short corollary after P13. The nonlinear period is T(ε), with limit 2π/|ωH|; it need not equal that limit along the entire branch. Distinguish gcd(m,n) nodes per phase cluster from n/gcd(m,n) distinct phases.</p></article>
''')

sec('part4','PART IV — Deep audits of P05, P08, P10 and P11',r'''
<h3>P05: how much of the selection atlas survives an adversarial comparison?</h3>
<p><strong>KNOWN RESULT:</strong> finite-ring spectra can be optimized on roots of unity, with a particularly close quadratic-cosine example in Lorenz-96. <strong>OUR RESULT:</strong> a moving positive vertex and a complete physical parameter atlas. <strong>MINIMAL DIFFERENCE:</strong> the variable vertex, sparse law and physical lift. The elementary optimization is a small difference; the parameter realization and its relation to criticality are structurally meaningful.</p>
<p>The reduction itself explains why the nearest <em>cosine</em> rule is routine. For D=H−G&gt;0,</p>
<div class="math">r(u)=d+D+C²/(8D)−2D(u−z)², &nbsp; z=C/(4D)&gt;0.<br>r(uⱼ)−r(uⱼ₊₁)=C(uⱼ−uⱼ₊₁)(1−χβⱼ), &nbsp; χ=4D/C.</div>
<p>This is one-dimensional nearest-point geometry. The midpoint equation establishes adjacency of maximal ties; three distinct cosines cannot all be equidistant from one real vertex. Once z is restricted to the positive half-line, the endpoint count J and its residues modulo four follow from the cosine grid. Calling this a new spectral optimization technique would be too strong. Calling it a complete exact classification for this constrained model is defensible.</p>
<p>The stronger statement is constructive: prescribed z,x,c,g determine h, while k can be chosen to ensure positive a through the equilibrium relation. The regular physical chart then transfers strict inequalities to open parameter regions and ties to smooth hypersurfaces. This solves a feasibility problem left out of an unconstrained dispersion plot. Stewart’s arbitrary-order theorem does not imply it: allowing every connection coefficient independently is a different parameter class. {B12}</p>
<p>Stable switching deserves a short proposition, not a headline. Adding sufficiently strong negative diagonal contribution can make an entire finite family of modes stable while their ordering changes. In DM that diagonal change must be accompanied by the correct equilibrium-preserving adjustment of a. The construction is useful evidence that the selection atlas is not itself a bifurcation diagram.</p>
<h3>P11: the nontrivial step is the image of the positive parameter domain</h3>
<p><strong>KNOWN RESULT:</strong> an admissible Hopf point requires spectral conditions and feasible parameters; this distinction is explicit in the circulant evolutionary example cited above. <strong>OUR RESULT:</strong> eliminate the DM physical coefficients sufficiently to decide an entire strict selection cell. <strong>MINIMAL DIFFERENCE:</strong> a necessary-and-sufficient inequality depending only on the discrete mode geometry, with constructive lifting. That is a meaningful theorem-level difference, although exact-model priority is unresolved.</p>
<p>At prescribed equilibrium amplitude x and vertex z, the blueprint rewrites the candidate condition as</p>
<div class="math">kx = D wⱼ − C[1−uⱼ+x/(1+x)],<br>D=C/(4z), &nbsp; wⱼ=1−2uⱼ².<br>k&gt;0 ⇔ wⱼ/(4z)&gt;1−uⱼ+x/(1+x).</div>
<p>This inequality exposes what an arbitrary diagonal shift would hide: the physical k cannot be negative. Letting x range over positive values and intersecting the admissible z-interval with the strict Voronoi cell produces</p>
<div class="math">wⱼ&gt;0 and Lⱼ&lt;Bⱼ, &nbsp; Lⱼ=max(0,βⱼ), &nbsp; Bⱼ=wⱼ/[4(1−uⱼ)].</div>
<p>Sufficiency also needs positive a and nonzero frequency. The blueprint supplies those by choosing G large enough and excluding at most one zero-frequency value. Thus this is more than a necessary inequality derived from a dispersion relation. The pair is individually simple because all other representatives have strictly smaller real parts and its two values are distinct when ωⱼ≠0. That last implication is routine; the positive lift is the substantive analysis.</p>
<p>The two explicit failures are different. For (8,1), wⱼ=0 precludes positive k at zero abscissa. For (12,2), wⱼ&gt;0 but the lower strict cell endpoint equals Bⱼ=1/4, so the required open intersection is empty. A table that records only wⱼ&gt;0 would miss the second obstruction. These cells can still dominate, including at stable equilibria. That concrete separation is more persuasive than claiming that positivity restrictions themselves are new.</p>
<p>Angeli–Banaji–Pantea use additive-compound and signed-graph structure to exclude Hopf in broader interaction classes. Their existence shows that obstruction theory is established; it does not furnish this sharp Fourier-cell criterion. Conversely, merely citing their framework would not prove that DM’s iff is already known more generally. {B24}</p>
<p>The exact five-node tuple is a clean bridge to the nonlinear work: xH=1, (c,gH,h)=(2,16,6), kH=(3√5−5)/4 and aH=(47+3√5)/4. Its role is to exhibit an admissible simple critical pair with a quantified stable complement. Its algebraic elegance does not by itself confer major novelty, and its existence is still distinct from a proven bifurcation along g.</p>
<h3>P08: an exact comparison, with a narrower interpretation than the slogan</h3>
<p><strong>KNOWN RESULT:</strong> circulant bilinear systems admit spectral and Lyapunov analysis; lattice behavior can depend on whether coupling is diffusive or signalling-based. <strong>OUR RESULT:</strong> same nominal tuple, these two explicitly specified laws, exact all-size witnesses and residue/branch obstructions. <strong>MINIMAL DIFFERENCE:</strong> the matching constraint and sharp quantifiers. The general qualitative observation is old; these precise classifications appear new. {B19} {B27}</p>
<div class="math">DM: ∂Fᵢ/∂xᵢ₊q = +gxᵢ/(1+xᵢ₊q)².<br>MB: ∂Fᵢ/∂yᵢ₊q = −gyᵢ.</div>
<p>Consequently this is not a comparison at fixed signed Jacobian topology. DM also injects cxᵢ₋₁ at a zero receiver, while MB leaves coordinate faces invariant. The result is still mathematically useful: identical directed dependencies and coefficients do not determine dynamics when the interaction formula is unspecified. What it does not isolate is saturation while keeping every regulatory sign, scale and boundary condition fixed.</p>
<p>The universal forward example is stronger than a numerical pair of simulations. At (a,k,c,g,h)=(1/4,1/8,1,1,1/8), both positive equilibria equal one, DM has abscissa −1/4 and MB has a positive nonuniform eigenvalue for every n≥5 with q=2. Strictness yields open parameter regions separately for each fixed n. Do not silently replace that quantifier by a single uniform neighborhood valid for infinitely many sizes unless an additional uniform-margin argument is supplied.</p>
<p>The reverse theorem must be split. On C₊={M&gt;0,μ&gt;0}, with M=k+g+h−c, both positive branches are uniquely named, and reverse stability occurs iff n≡3 mod 4. On the broader branch-labelled domain, a lower DM root can be uniformly unstable for branch reasons; the obstruction changes to 4∣n. Those lower-branch examples are less compelling as evidence for a <em>spatial</em> selection effect. They belong after the unique-root theorem, with the mechanism made explicit.</p>
<p>The independent stable-mode realization is particularly valuable. It fixes the same tuple while permitting any ordered pair of reachable strict representatives, even when both equilibria are stable. That says the two selection maps are not locked together by the matching constraint. It is logically different from, and stronger in a different direction than, one stability reversal.</p>
<h3>P10: a special identity followed by standard local theory</h3>
<p><strong>KNOWN RESULT:</strong> a simple zero and nonzero unfolding/quadratic coefficients yield a full-system fold; a transverse stable spectrum gives the usual local sink–saddle organization. <strong>OUR RESULT:</strong> those transverse inequalities are automatic for every canonical positive fold, regardless of n and q. <strong>MINIMAL DIFFERENCE:</strong> an equation-specific identity removes extra assumptions. This is a worthwhile supporting theorem, not a new center-manifold theory. {B34}</p>
<div class="math">Gf−Hf=rf(k+c/sf²)&gt;0,<br>rₘ(f)=Cf(cosθₘ−1)+(Gf−Hf)(cos(qθₘ)−1)&lt;0 for m≠0.</div>
<p>The second line is a standard trigonometric sign argument once the first line is available. The step-one term makes it strict even if q and n are not coprime. This is a symmetric-part spectral observation; it does not establish global cooperativity of the nonlinear vector field. The explicit sufficient interval for coexistence can be chosen independently of dimension, but the displayed transverse spectral gap shrinks as n increases. Do not infer a uniform-in-n spectral gap or an n-independent index-one neighborhood from the local proof alone.</p>
<p>The branch diagram is a familiar persistent-origin/backward-branch/fold arrangement. Its special strength is full-system certification near the fold. The example n=6,q=1 with (a,k,c,g,h)=(6,1/10,1/10,101/10,9/10) is an important limiting fact: the upper root x=4 has a positive Nyquist eigenvalue despite negative uniform eigenvalue; the lower root has more than one unstable direction. This decisively blocks a whole-interval claim. The exact threshold on synchrony and the local stable disk are useful descriptions, but neither classifies the full global basin boundary.</p>
<h3>What the three-stage package does—and does not—establish</h3>
<table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Current answer</th><th>Remaining boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Which Fourier representatives can dominate?</td><td>P05: exact cells, ties and positive realizations.</td><td>Dominance may describe the slowest decaying mode at a stable equilibrium.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Which strict dominant cells can be critical?</td><td>P11: exact physical iff, excluding frequency-zero choices.</td><td>No designated-path crossing or periodic orbit yet.</td></tr>
<tr><td>How does the answer depend on the law?</td><td>P08: exact DM–MB stability and selected-mode comparisons.</td><td>No matched MB nonlinear Hopf criticality theorem yet.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>No inspected source supplied this full three-stage package for the specified matched laws. That absence supports a conditional apparent-novelty judgment for the combination; it does not establish that the conceptual decomposition has never been used elsewhere. More significantly, the <em>current package</em> is not yet a complete two-law classification of Hopf criticality: P08 compares selection and stability, while P11’s detailed candidate criterion is DM-specific.</p>
''')

sec('part5','PART V — Future literature risk: P12–P14',r'''
<h3>P12: necessary exact verification, limited independent novelty</h3>
<p>The intended path fixes a,k,c,h and varies g. The equilibrium moves. The correct calculation starts from E(x,g)=a−(k+h)x+(c−g)/(1+x)=0 and differentiates E along the chosen smooth branch. Then differentiate rⱼ(x(g),g). An equilibrium-coordinate derivative such as ∂ₖRⱼ=−x is a different direction because maintaining that chart also changes a. A generic perturbation theorem is likewise insufficient for the prescribed physical path. {B13} {B33}</p>
<p><strong>Recommended target:</strong> exact crossing sign at the algebraic candidate; a rigorously specified nearby interval retaining positivity, branch identity, strict mode selection and stable complement; an explicit statement of the unstable side. This is projected ROUTINE APPLICATION, HIGH confidence / LOW methodological priority risk. A theorem for a whole admissible family of crossings would be more useful but should not delay the essential pointwise calculation. Neither target is presently proved.</p>
<h3>P13: pursue interpretation of the sign, not expression length</h3>
<p>The likely computation is efficient because translation symmetry imposes mode addition modulo n. The quadratic terms feeding the Hopf coefficient pass through modes 0 and 2m, and the cubic term returns to m. Existing ring papers already exploit this. DM’s rational terms contribute genuine higher derivatives; they prevent simply copying a quadratic-model coefficient. Normalize the Fourier vectors, Taylor factorials, adjoint inner product and positive-frequency choice before comparing formulas. {B11} {B33}</p>
<p>Comparable work has gone beyond a single sign check: biological repression models obtain criticality conditions involving derivatives of the feedback function, and circulant evolutionary models express criticality across parameter families. These are appropriate standards for an ambitious P13 result. {B21} {B23}</p>
<ol><li><strong>Minimum useful completion:</strong> an exact nonzero l₁ at P11’s five-node candidate, plus P12, yields a rigorous local nonlinear existence/classification theorem. This materially improves completeness, but contributes limited standalone novelty.</li>
<li><strong>Preferred target:</strong> a tractable formula on the admissible Hopf surface and a proved sign on an explicitly described substantial region, with n/mode restrictions. A modest formula with a strong inequality is better than a very long algebraic expression without interpretation.</li>
<li><strong>Stronger connection:</strong> relate criticality to the P11 cell constraints—e.g. determine whether all candidates in a named mode family share one sign, or exhibit rigorously separated sign regimes within the admissible domain.</li>
<li><strong>Optional law comparison:</strong> compare DM and MB on explicitly defined Hopf surfaces or crossing paths. Same nominal coefficients need not put both models at Hopf simultaneously; do not compare an l₁ at Hopf with an off-boundary quantity.</li></ol>
<p>An unspecified open neighborhood with fixed sign follows automatically from a nonzero value and continuity. A meaningful region theorem needs explicit domain, parameter margins or size/mode coverage. An exact closed form is useful for reproducibility but is not a substitute for such a theorem. A zero of l₁ supplies only a possible generalized-Hopf location; proving a Bautin bifurcation requires the additional higher-order and unfolding conditions.</p>
<h3>P14: exact symmetry is standard, its physically realized instance is useful</h3>
<p>After the simple nondegenerate branch exists, cyclic equivariance determines its spatiotemporal symmetry. With the blueprint’s signed eigenvalue convention, the anticipated adjacent-node relation is xᵢ₊₁(t)=xᵢ(t+sgn(ωₘ)mT(ε)/n), modulo the nonlinear period and subject to a consistent choice of Fourier representative. There are n/gcd(m,n) distinct phases, with gcd(m,n) nodes in each phase class. The leading cosine and the exact shift identity are different assertions. {B12} {B16}</p>
<p><strong>Recommended target:</strong> a short corollary stating the actual nonlinear period, signed shift, spatial kernel and orbital stability inherited from the Hopf theorem. Do not say that the graph’s arrow orientation alone determines wave direction. Do not claim constant period 2π/ωH away from the bifurcation. Generic rotating waves add little novelty; the novel content can be which positive-parameter mode cells support a proved stable or unstable DM wave.</p>
''')

