sec('part9','PART IX — Claims that could be strengthened',r'''
<p><strong>Emphasize quantifiers.</strong> State P08’s forward witness for every n≥5, the exact residue-class iff on C₊, and the separately labelled lower-branch result. State P11’s necessity and constructive sufficiency, not just its excluded examples. State P05’s transfer from effective coefficients to positive physical parameters and open sets. These are stronger than a collection of parameter scans.</p>
<p><strong>Make P10’s generality visible.</strong> The fold conclusion is valid for all allowed offsets, including coincident and noncoprime cases. Attribute this to the step-one term and the exact fold identity. Keep the dimension-independent sufficient coexistence interval separate from the dimension-dependent transverse gap.</p>
<p><strong>A useful additional deduction for P08, not an archived verified claim.</strong> The MB strict spectral condition can be strengthened to global asymptotic stability of the positive equilibrium <em>within the strictly positive orthant</em>. This is a standard generalized Lotka–Volterra Lyapunov specialization, not a new general theorem. {B18} {B19}</p>
<div class="math">B=−kI+cP−gP⁻q−hPq, &nbsp; y*=a/(k+g+h−c),<br>V(y)=Σᵢ[yᵢ−y*−y*log(yᵢ/y*)], &nbsp; z=y−y*1.<br>V̇ = zᵀBz ≤ −δ‖z‖², &nbsp; δ=k−Kₙ,q(c,g+h)&gt;0.</div>
<p>Indeed ẏ=diag(y)(a1+By), B(y*1)=−a1, and the symmetric part of the real circulant B has largest eigenvalue −δ. V tends to infinity as any coordinate approaches zero or infinity, so a positive trajectory stays in a compact interior sublevel set and converges to y*1. This derivation is supplied by this audit for subsequent project verification. It cannot be extended to every boundary initial condition: MB coordinate faces are invariant and cannot converge to a fully positive equilibrium from a missing species.</p>
<p>If incorporated and checked, the reverse P08 comparison becomes stronger: an unstable DM homogeneous equilibrium can be paired with an MB equilibrium globally attracting in the positive interior. The strengthening uses established theory; its value is in sharpening the exact law comparison. Likewise the independent stable-mode theorem could contrast local DM stability with global interior MB attraction.</p>
''')

sec('part10','PART X — Literature gaps worth exploiting',r'''
<ol><li><strong>Resolve source priority before broadening scope.</strong> Obtain the Lin proceedings, full journal text and thesis. This can change whether the candidate/Hopf calculations are new, even if it leaves the general-n classifications intact.</li>
<li><strong>Unify the selection and criticality figures.</strong> For each n, show the positive vertex cells and the intersection with z&lt;Bⱼ, then add the amplitude-dependent boundary from P11. Clearly mark dominance-only regions. This is a theorem visualization, not evidence in place of proof.</li>
<li><strong>Give a usable nonlinear theorem on an explicit admissible domain.</strong> Derive P13 on the physical Hopf surface, then prove a sign result with stated size/mode and parameter restrictions. Tie it to a meaningful part of the P11 atlas.</li>
<li><strong>Separate the two reverse-comparison mechanisms.</strong> In the unique-root domain the residue obstruction reflects spatial spectral geometry. On the lower branch, uniform branch instability can drive the contrast. This separation improves both novelty and interpretation without adding a new research direction.</li>
<li><strong>Use the algebraic five-node point as a complete worked theorem.</strong> Finish P12, one exact l₁ and P14 there even if a larger sign theorem follows later. A spectral candidate is a weaker deliverable than a fully certified nonlinear example.</li>
<li><strong>Keep asymptotics subordinate.</strong> P06 can explain the finite-ring results, but a standard sampling limit should not displace exact finite-size obstructions.</li></ol>
<p>These targets stay within P01–P14. A generic first-Hopf calculation for an unrelated ring, more simulation cases, or a longer list of fifth-root identities would add little to the current contribution.</p>
''')

sec('part11','PART XI — Recommended manuscript positioning',r'''
<p><strong>Recommended title:</strong> <em>Finite-ring mode selection and physical criticality constraints in demand-modulated cyclic networks</em>.</p>
<p><strong>Contribution paragraph, using only current results:</strong> We study homogeneous equilibria of a directed cyclic network with receiver-dependent input and compare them with a specified bilinear network sharing the same directed dependency support and nominal coefficients. For step-two coupling, we classify the complete finite-size dominant-mode cells and prove their realization by positive parameters. We then derive a necessary-and-sufficient criterion for a strict nonuniform cell to contain a physical spectral critical point, identifying selectable modes that cannot become critical. Matched-law comparisons yield exact stability reversals, residue-class and branch restrictions, and independent realization of dominant modes at stable equilibria. Full-system transcritical and saddle-node analysis organizes the synchronous branches, including automatic transverse stability at every positive fold. These results distinguish spectral ordering, admissible criticality and interaction-law dependence; nonlinear Hopf and rotating-wave conclusions require the additional calculations specified separately.</p>
<p><strong>Six principal contributions:</strong></p>
<ol><li>The complete finite-n q=2 selection atlas with physical cell and switch realization.</li>
<li>The exact P11 positivity-constrained candidate criterion and explicit obstruction cells.</li>
<li>The all-n same-tuple forward contrast and the exact reverse parity/branch distinctions.</li>
<li>Independent realization of every reachable ordered pair of dominant representatives with both models stable.</li>
<li>The universal transverse-fold identity and a certified open region of coexistence.</li>
<li>The exact five-node spectral candidate, to be upgraded to a nonlinear Hopf/wave theorem only after P12–P14.</li></ol>
<p><strong>Main text:</strong> model/comparator definition, concise framework, P05–P11 geometric relationship, P08 comparisons, branch-labelled P09/P10 organization, counterexamples and one five-node illustration. Give the closest-prior differences explicitly in the introduction.</p>
<p><strong>Appendices:</strong> extended P01 edge cases, complete negative-root tables, Jacobian collision bookkeeping, full P07 ordering/coincidence table, long constructive algebra, future tensor derivatives and normalization checks. P06 belongs here unless it reveals a genuinely additional phenomenon.</p>
<p><strong>Not novelty:</strong> a Fourier eigenbasis, invariant synchrony, generic local bifurcation theorems, the textbook l₁ formula or the equivariant Hopf conclusion. Use citations and short supporting statements.</p>
<h3>Strongest defensible claim after P12–P14 are actually proved</h3>
<p>A completed project could claim <strong>an exact finite-size classification of physically attainable dominant and critical modes for the specified demand-modulated law, sharp same-tuple contrasts with its bilinear comparator, and a rigorously classified nonlinear rotating-wave realization connected to that classification</strong>. If P13 covers only one point, the last phrase must mean one local realization, not a global wave atlas or all admissible candidates.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Source of value</th><th>What it contributes</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>A — P05/P08/P10/P11, current</td><td>The apparent novelty already lies in constrained mode feasibility, exact matched-law comparisons and the structural fold identity. It remains subject to exact-model priority access.</td></tr>
<tr><td>B — P12, future</td><td>Certifies that the designated physical path crosses the boundary. Necessary local dynamical meaning; little independent novelty.</td></tr>
<tr><td>C — P13, future</td><td>Converts a linear candidate into a nonlinear bifurcation with stability/type. A substantial sign theorem adds more than one nonzero value.</td></tr>
<tr><td>D — P14, future</td><td>Identifies the exact cyclic phase pattern and clusters of the resulting branch using established symmetry theory. Mainly interpretation and completion.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Direct answer about one-point l₁:</strong> yes, it materially strengthens the paper’s completeness by proving and classifying an actual local periodic branch; no, it does not by itself materially strengthen the novelty of the general method. The preferred further target is a sign classification on an explicit admissible Hopf region with a size/mode or interaction-law consequence. Exact closed form is a means to that target. An unspecified continuity neighborhood is too weak; a fully matched criticality comparison can be valuable but needs separately certified MB Hopf data.</p>
''')

